f. community participation; andg. funding.

Article 20

The obligations and responsibilities of Community Institutions and Civil Society Organizations as referred to in Article 15 letter d include: a. providing information and/or reporting every occurrence of vulnerability and violence risks that they know of;
b. providing protection for women and children;
c. providing emergency assistance;
d. accompanying victims, providing shelters and/or safe houses initiated by community institutions or civil society organizations; and e. assisting in the process of repatriation, social rehabilitation and social reintegration.

Article 23

1. The Regional Government shall establish partnerships with the business sector in the protection of women and children from acts of violence. (2) Partnerships as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out through: a. providing financial support in the prevention and handling of cases of violence against women and children;
b. providing employment opportunities for women victims of violence; and c. education and training for women and children victims of violence.

Article 10

1. In order to maximize the function and role of UPT PPA, the agency in charge of Women Empowerment and Children as the parent of UPT PPA may establish TRC which is determined through a Regent's decree.

7 - (2) The membership of TRC as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of: a. paralegals;
b. women and children activists;
c. health workers;
d. mass media; and/ore. observers of vulnerable groups.
3. Requirements for TRC members namely:a. Indonesian citizens proven by population identity; b. have a good perspective on women and children;
c. physically and mentally healthy; andd. have good communication skills, preferably those who are able to use sign language.
4. TRC as referred to in paragraph (1) has the duties and functions of conducting outreach and victim assistance.
5. TRC reports the results of task implementation to the head of the Agency in charge of women and children through the head of UPT PPA.

Article 16

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 15 letter a include: a. establishing policies for the protection of women and children; b. implementing policies for the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
c. cooperating with all parties in the implementation of protection of women and children;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children, in accordance with regional financial capacity;
f. encouraging the role of the private sector and community institutions to be involved in supporting the protection of women and children;

9 -
g. encouraging the establishment of local institutions at the sub-district and/or village level to provide protection for women and children; and h. encouraging the establishment of regulations at the village level governing the protection of women and children;
i. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children; and j. realizing a Child-Friendly Regency.
2. In addition to the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government has the obligation and responsibility to provide special protection to Children.
3. Special Protection for Children as referred to in paragraph (2) is provided to: a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are exploited economically and/or sexually including child marriage;
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic substances, and other addictive substances abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with Human Immunodeficiency Virus/Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome;
h. children who are victims of kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking; i. children who are victims of physical and/or psychological violence;
j. children who are victims of sexual crimes;
k. children who are victims of terrorist networks;
l. children with disabilities;
m. children who are victims of abuse and neglect;
n. children with deviant social behavior; and o. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
4. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in this Article shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus or appointed Officials.

Article 18

The obligations and responsibilities of the Village Government as referred to in Article 15 letter b include:
a. establishing policies for the protection of women and children at the village level; b. implementing policies for the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
c. cooperating with all parties in the implementation of protection of women and children;

10 -
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children, in accordance with village financial capacity;
f. involving community institutions at the village level in supporting the protection of women and children;
g. creating institutions at the village level to provide protection for women and children;
h. formulating regulations at the village level governing the protection of women and children; and i. realizing a women-friendly and child-caring village.

Article 3

The Implementation of Women's and Children's Protection aims to: a. prevent acts of violence against women and children; b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protect, provide a sense of security for women and children; d. provide services to women victims of violence, reporters, and witnesses;
e. provide services to children both as victims, witnesses and as perpetrators of violence;
f. facilitate and mediate household disputes to realize an equal and just household; and g. prioritize services and protection for Vulnerable Groups.

Article 27

1. Funding for the implementation of protection for women and children includes: a. prevention of violence against women and children;
b. service institutions including service facilities and infrastructure; c. medical services, which include doctor examinations, treatment costs, hospital costs, medication costs and medical support costs; d. medicolegal services, which include examinations for visum et repertum, visum et repertum psychiatricum, and Deoxyribonucleic Acid tests; e. psychosocial services, consisting of counseling and psychological therapy, safe houses and safe house management;
f. legal services and assistance including costs of assistance at the police, prosecutor's office, and court;
g. operational costs of service institutions, including outreach transportation, assistance transportation, and fulfillment of victim needs while at the service institution; and h. service institution officers, including honorarium and capacity building.
2. Management of funding as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 12

1. The Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children has the duties to: a. promote the Protection of Women and Children to the community on an ongoing basis;
b. carry out socialization and advocacy of the Protection of Women and Children to government officials and the community;
c. prepare a regional action plan for the Protection of Women and Children; d. prepare an annual work program;
e. encourage the realization of planning and budgeting that supports the Protection of Women and children; andf. formulate recommendations for regent's policies.
2. The Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) is responsible to the Regent.

